What is the difference in output between these?
A: SELECT * FROM a INNER JOIN b ON a.id = b.a_id;
B: SELECT * FROM a LEFT JOIN b ON a.id = b.a_id WHERE b.a_id IS NOT NULL;

Two tables with the same schema: apr_sales and may_sales. Each has product_id and revenue. You want all products from both months. Best approach?
A: SELECT * FROM apr_sales UNION SELECT * FROM may_sales;
B: SELECT * FROM apr_sales FULL OUTER JOIN may_sales ON apr_sales.product_id = may_sales.product_id;

A self-join query finds employees and their managers:
SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager FROM employees e LEFT JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id;

If an employee has no manager, what is returned?

Performance question: You have 1M users and 100M orders. Which JOIN order is more efficient?
A: SELECT * FROM users JOIN orders ON users.id = orders.user_id WHERE orders.status = 'completed';
B: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'completed' JOIN users ON users.id = orders.user_id;

A query with multiple LEFT JOINs:
SELECT u.id FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id LEFT JOIN payments p ON o.id = p.order_id WHERE p.status = 'failed';

What rows are included if an order has no payments?
